## Step 2 — implement
Write `work/w3_atomic-claim/e2e_claim.sh`: a literal, self-contained bash script
that builds nothing itself but runs the binary the acceptance step builds, in a
scratch store under its own `mktemp -d`, cleaning that directory up on every exit
path (trap). It prints `E2E_PASS` as the LAST line only when every step passed, and
exits non-zero otherwise.

Hard boundaries — this script runs on a shared host:
- Never touch the fleet store, `~/.local/bin/bd`, or any live daemon. Pass
  `--no-daemon`, or point every command at your own temp store, throughout.
- Never call `bd daemon --stop-all` or any other command that affects another
  workspace.
- Clean up your temp directory even when a step fails.

Step 2 of the script is the whole point of this work item: two concurrent claims on
one open issue must produce exactly one exit 0 and one exit 3, with the stored
assignee equal to the winner. That is the probe scenario that failed before this item
(both claimants exited 0 and one claim was silently lost). Make the script assert it,
not merely run it, and print the literal line `one exit=0, one exit=3`.

